0 194 315 [
[(This patch demonstrates using ring modulation to alias a sound down)]
[(one octave. The ring modulation itself \050[osc~] and multiplier\051 is)]
[(easy. \050We step it up by a factor of 2 to balance the original better.\051)]
] 14 -0 0 0 false DrawText
grestore
gsave
/Menlo-Regular findfont 12 scalefont ISOEncode setfont
0.000 0.000 0.000 setrgbcolor AdjustColor
0 194 263 [
[(Harder is getting the fundamental frequency of the original sound. We)]
[(do this with the complicated [sigmund~] object, which puts out a)]
[(stream of analysis data for an incoming signal. The "-npts 2048")]
[(argument specifies the analysis window size. The analysis is most)]
[(closely aligned with what the sound was doing at the middle of the)]
[(window, i.e., 1024 samples ago. The [pd delay] window delays the)]
[(signal itself 1024 samples so it will be as tightly synchronized with)]
[(the analysis data as possible. \050If you're doing this on a real-time)]
[(input, you might drop the delay and settle for less perfect)]
[(synchronization.\051)]
] 14 -0 0 0 false DrawText
grestore
gsave
/Menlo-Regular findfont 12 scalefont ISOEncode setfont
0.000 0.000 0.000 setrgbcolor AdjustColor
0 195 111 [
[(About [sigmund~], suffice it to say that the left outlet, by default,)]
[(outputs pitch of the analyzed sound. When it fails to find a pitch,)]
[([sigmund~] outputs -1500, so we use [moses] to filter these values.)]
[(The pitch is converted from MIDI to Hertz, and multiplied by 1/2 to)]
[(control the modulation oscillator. \050You can also try large-ish)]
[(integers which leave the pitch intact but introduce funny formants.\051)]
] 14 -0 0 0 false DrawText
grestore
gsave
